  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate resilience and agri-finance</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">4 Micro-finance institutions</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Microfinance is a powerful instrument to achieve positive environmental, social, and financial impacts by providing access to capital in low-income economies, which can improve the livelihoods of populations at the bottom of the pyramid, enabling them to build assets, increase incomes, and reduce their vulnerability to economic and environmental stress.
Smallholder farmers and MSMEs need green finance to absorb shocks and take advantage of opportunities but face a wide range of constraints in accessing this finance. Developing or transitioning to climate-friendly business models requires capital investments that smallholder farmers and MSMEs are unlikely to afford. Currently, accessing finance is constrained by issues that have historically faced smallholder farmers and MSMEs, such as strict collateral requirements, and those specific to green finance, such as higher perceived risk.
While financiers and development actors are working to address constraints, there remains little clarity on the extent to which green finance reaches smallholder farmers and MSMEs and how the flows can be expanded. Despite extensive data on climate finance flows and MSME financing, there is limited capacity on the side of MFIs in the Rwandan context on green finance flows, giving sense and reason to such Rabo Foundation intervention. 
According to Climate Resilience Through Microfinance study, conducted by Helwig et al. (2023) available in this link, financial mitigation and diversification among the farmers who indicated a climate and 
environmental impact as the most important problem affecting their household, over 80% admitted that the issue caused a reduction in income. In these cases, less than 3% fully recovered and close to 30% not having recovered, at all, with the rest having recovered only partially. 
This Rabo Foundation and Cordaid partnership will close the gap and sustain green finance by including 
financially smallholders&#x2019; farmers with the special attention on climate change impact. The approach is to support both supply and demand sides to identify the gaps and work together in finding sustainable solutions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Providing technical assistance to 4 MFIs to become climate finance ready, enabling them to offer climate sensitive and resilient financial services to 5000 farmers as well as creating a framework for replicability of climate resilience product development for MFIs.</narrative>
  </description>
